  "abstract": "The Hobart–Brouwer table lists the parameter set $(63,22,14,5,9)$, and with it the complementary set $(63,40,32,26,24)$, as unresolved. We construct directed strongly regular graphs with both parameter sets. The 63-vertex graph has two layers. The first is the Cayley digraph $\\Gamma_{36}$ of $\\mathbb Z_3\\times A_4$ with parameters $(36,13,11,2,6)$ from rStation paper 79. The second is the undirected srg$(27,10,1,5)$ on the 27 lines of a cubic surface, two lines being adjacent when they meet. The arcs from $\\Gamma_{36}$ to the lines come from two partitions: the cosets of $\\{0\\}\\times A_4$, and a partition of the lines into three 9-sets, each met by every outside line in exactly 3 lines. The arcs back to $\\Gamma_{36}$ then only have to satisfy linear conditions. We give a 0/1 solution explicitly. It is invariant under a fixed-point-free automorphism of order 3, so a table of 9 rows determines it. Unlike the couplings of rStation papers 4 and 79, the back arcs do not come from partitions, and they mix both eigenspaces. We found eight such graphs, pairwise non-isomorphic, none isomorphic to its converse, each with automorphism group of order 3. So each of the two rows has at least 16 isomorphism classes. Papers 79 and 81 constructed the line $(9m,3m+1,m+7,m-2,m+2)$ for every $m\\ge4$ except $m=7$, and $m=7$ is the new row. Hence a directed strongly regular graph with these parameters exists if and only if $m\\ge4$. A standard-library script rebuilds the graph from the printed data and checks it by two methods. A second script, sharing no code with the first, checks all 16 stored matrices.",
